    edge((3, 0), (3, 1), "--|>"),
    edge((4, 0), (3, 1), "--|>"),
  ),
)

The engine owns the arena, the report sink, and the limits. It hands the reader
a `Builder` and the input; it freezes the result into a `Document`; it runs the
filter pipeline, each stage producing a new `Document` in the same arena; it
hands the last one to the writer. It never inspects a node, never looks at a
tag, and never knows which plugins it called.

Two properties follow from the AST sitting between the halves. Each reader and
each writer is compiled once regardless of how many formats exist, so the
conversion matrix does not multiply code size — only the small dispatch glue is
quadratic. And a reader can be tested against the AST directly, with no writer
involved, which is what the tree-test suite does.

= The Document AST

== Shape: pandoc's model

The node set is deliberately close to `Text.Pandoc.Definition`. Where a
construct exists in pandoc it keeps pandoc's name and meaning, so a reader of
one system can read the other, and so a future pandoc-JSON bridge is a
translation rather than a redesign.

```zig
pub const BlockTag = enum(u8) {
    // Leaf blocks: content is inline.
    plain,             // a paragraph that must not be wrapped in <p>
    para,
    line_block,        // preserved line breaks, as in poetry
    heading,           // pandoc's Header
    code_block,        // content is raw text, not inlines
    raw_block,         // format-specific literal
    horizontal_rule,

    // Container blocks: content is blocks.
    block_quote,
    div,               // pandoc's Div: a generic attributed container
    figure,
    bullet_list,
    ordered_list,
    list_item,
    definition_list,
    definition_item,
    definition_term,   // content is inline
    definition_body,
    table,
    caption,
    table_head,
    table_body,
    table_foot,
    table_row,
    table_cell,
};

pub const InlineTag = enum(u8) {
    // Leaf inlines.
    str,               // a run of text containing no whitespace
    space,
    soft_break,
    line_break,
    code,              // inline code; content is raw text
    math,              // attr selects inline or display
    raw_inline,

    // Container inlines: content is inlines.
    emph,
    strong,
    strikeout,
    superscript,
    subscript,
    small_caps,
    underline,
    quoted,            // attr selects single or double
    link,
    image,             // children are the alt text
    span,              // pandoc's Span: a generic attributed container
    cite,

    // Container inline whose content is blocks.
    note,              // pandoc's Note: a footnote body
};
```

Three points about this list are decisions rather than transcription.

`space`, `soft_break`, and `line_break` are separate leaf nodes rather than
characters inside a text run, exactly as in pandoc. This costs nodes and buys
the ability for a filter to distinguish a line wrap in the source from a
deliberate break, and for a writer to re-wrap text without guessing.

`div` and `span` exist so a format's structure that has no direct node — an
AsciiDoc admonition, an HTML `section`, a DOCX content control, an RST
directive — survives as an attributed container rather than being flattened.
They are the extension point that keeps the node set closed, they are what most
filters match on, and they are in the set from the start rather than added when
the first such format arrives.

`emph` and `strong` are containers, not flags. This reverses an earlier draft;
the reasoning is under _Why the tree is a tree_.

== Storage: flat, preorder, struct-of-arrays

The tree is stored as two `std.MultiArrayList` arrays — one of blocks, one of
inlines — each holding its nodes in document preorder, each node recording the
index one past its last descendant.

```zig
pub const Range = struct {
    start: u32,
    len: u32,

    pub const empty: Range = .{ .start = 0, .len = 0 };

    pub fn end(r: Range) u32 {
        return r.start + r.len;
    }
};

pub const Block = struct {
    tag: BlockTag,
    /// Tag-specific payload. Never read directly; the encoding is defined
    /// once, in attr.zig, and reached through accessors.
    attr: u32,
    /// Index into `Document.attrs` for the (id, classes, pairs) triple, or
    /// `none` when the tag carries no attributes.
    attrs: u32,
    /// Inline children, for tags whose content is inline. Container blocks
    /// hold `Range.empty`.
    inlines: Range,
    /// One past this node's last descendant block. A leaf at index `i` has
    /// `subtree_end == i + 1`.
    subtree_end: u32,
};

pub const Inline = struct {
    tag: InlineTag,
    attr: u32,
    attrs: u32,
    /// Meaning depends on the tag: a text range for `str`, `code`, `math`,
    /// and `raw_inline`; a block range for `note`; empty otherwise.
    data: Range,
    /// One past this node's last descendant inline.
    subtree_end: u32,
};

pub const Document = struct {
    blocks: std.MultiArrayList(Block).Slice,
    inlines: std.MultiArrayList(Inline).Slice,
    /// The top-level block forest. Blocks outside this range are note bodies.
    body: Range,
    text: []const u8,
    attrs: []const Attr,
    strings: []const Range,
    links: []const Link,
    media: []const Media,
    meta: []const Meta,
};
```

Children of node `i` are found by hopping: start at `i + 1`, and from each
child `j` jump to `subtree_end[j]`, stopping at the parent's own
`subtree_end`. There is no child list to allocate and no parent pointer to
maintain.

```zig
pub const Children = struct {
    ends: []const u32,
    cursor: u32,
    bound: u32,

    pub fn init(doc: *const Document, parent: u32) Children {
        const ends = doc.blocks.items(.subtree_end);
        assert(parent < ends.len);
        return .{ .ends = ends, .cursor = parent + 1, .bound = ends[parent] };
    }

    pub fn next(it: *Children) ?u32 {
        assert(it.cursor <= it.bound);
        if (it.cursor == it.bound) return null;
        const index = it.cursor;
        it.cursor = it.ends[index];
        assert(it.cursor > index);
        return index;
    }
};
```

The three properties this layout is chosen for:

#tbl(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  table.header([*Property*], [*Consequence*]),
  [A subtree is a contiguous slice],
  [Copying an unchanged branch during a filter rebuild is one `memcpy` per
    array. This is the property the filter system is built on, and a
    pointer-based tree cannot have it.],

  [`subtree_end` skips a subtree in `O(1)`],
  [A filter that only cares about `link` nodes advances past whole untouched
    branches without visiting them.],

  [Struct-of-arrays puts every tag in one byte column],
  [`std.mem.indexOfScalar` over `blocks.items(.tag)` is a contiguous byte scan
    the compiler vectorizes. "Find every table in this document" is a
    `memchr`, not a tree walk.],
)

== Attributes

Every node that pandoc gives an `Attr` gets one here: an identifier, a class
list, and ordered key-value pairs.

```zig
pub const Attr = struct {
    id: Range,        // into text; empty when absent
    classes: Range,   // into strings, each a Range into text
    pairs: Range,     // into pairs, each two Ranges into text
};
```

Attributes carry heading identifiers, the `code_block` language as the first
class, `div` and `span` roles, and everything a format has that the node set
does not name. They are also how filters select: matching a class is the
idiomatic way to write a transform that applies to some nodes and not others.

`Block.attr` and `Inline.attr` remain a `u32` tag-specific payload for the
small enumerated things that would be wasteful as string attributes. The
encoding is defined once, in `attr.zig`, and reached only through accessors, so
the compiler checks the tag-to-payload relationship at every use. The
preliminary sketch called this field `extra` and left its meaning to
convention; that is how a field becomes folklore.

#tbl(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  table.header([*Tag*], [*`attr` encoding*]),
  [`heading`], [Level, 1 to 6.],
  [`ordered_list`],
  [Packed: bits 0..1 the number style (decimal, lower-alpha, upper-alpha,
    lower-roman, upper-roman), bits 2..3 the delimiter (period, paren,
    two-paren), bit 4 tight, bits 8..31 the start number.],
  [`bullet_list`], [Bit 0: tight.],
  [`code_block`], [Reserved; the language is the first class in `attrs`.],
  [`raw_block`, `raw_inline`], [The `Format` the content is literal for.],
  [`table_cell`], [Packed: bits 0..7 column span, bits 8..15 row span, bits 16..17 alignment.],
  [`table`], [Column count, set on close.],
  [`quoted`], [0 single, 1 double.],
  [`math`], [0 inline, 1 display.],
  [`link`, `image`], [Index into `Document.links` for target and title.],
  [`note`], [The note's ordinal.],
  [`str`, `code`, `emph`, `strong`, and the rest], [Zero. Checked by the validator.],
)

== The text pool and side tables

All text lives in one growable byte array; a `Range` into it is 8 bytes and
never dangles. Text is stored *decoded*: XML character references, RTF hex
escapes, and HTML entities are resolved by the reader, so no writer ever sees a
source format's escaping. Text is UTF-8, and readers of other encodings
transcode on the way in.

Repeated strings — class names, language tags, attribute keys — are interned
through a hash map that lives only during the build and is dropped when the
`Document` is frozen. Interning is what keeps the attribute machinery cheap: a
document with ten thousand `div` nodes carrying the same class stores that
class once.

```zig
pub const Link = struct {
    target: Range,
    title: Range,
};

pub const Media = struct {
    source: Range,   // path, URL, or archive entry name
    bytes: Range,    // extracted content; empty in the first release
    mime: Range,
};

pub const Meta = struct {
    key: Range,
    /// Metadata values are documents in miniature, so a value is a block
    /// range: a title with emphasis in it survives.
    value: Range,
};
```

== Invariants

The following hold for every `Document` handed to a filter or a writer. They
are checked by `ast.validate`, which runs on every conversion in `Debug` and
`ReleaseSafe`, after every filter stage, in every test, and on every fuzz
iteration. The validator is the oracle that makes reader and filter fuzzing
meaningful: a plugin that produces a structurally impossible tree fails even
when nothing crashes.

+ `subtree_end[i] > i` and `<= len`, in both arrays.
+ For any `j` in `(i, subtree_end[i])`, `subtree_end[j] <= subtree_end[i]`.
  Subtrees nest properly.
+ Every `Range` lies within the array it indexes.
+ Container tags hold `inlines == Range.empty`; leaf-content tags have
  `subtree_end == i + 1`.
+ Child tags appear only under their parent tag: `list_item` under a list,
  `table_row` under a table section, `table_cell` under `table_row`,
  `definition_term` and `definition_body` under `definition_item`.
+ A container inline has at least one child, except `image`, whose alt text may
  be empty.
+ `note.data` names a block range outside `body`, and every `note` ordinal is
  distinct.
+ Depth does not exceed `limits.max_depth` in either tree.
+ `attr` is zero for every tag whose row above says so.
+ The text pool is valid UTF-8, and no `str` node contains a whitespace
  character — whitespace is a `space`, `soft_break`, or `line_break` node.
+ No two adjacent siblings are both `str`; readers coalesce.

== A worked example

The Markdown fragment

```md
## The *quick* [brown](http://x) fox

- one
- two

  still two
```

produces the two trees below. Indentation shows the nesting that `subtree_end`
encodes; it is not stored.

#tbl(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, auto, auto, auto),
  align: (right, left, right, right, right),
  table.header([*i*], [*block*], [*attr*], [*subtree#linebreak()end*], [*inlines*]),
  [0], [`heading`], [2], [1], [0..9],
  [1], [`bullet_list`], [`tight`], [5], [—],
  [2], [`  list_item`], [0], [3], [—],
  [3], [`    plain`], [0], [4], [9..10],
  [4], [`  list_item`], [0], [7], [—],
  [5], [`    para`], [0], [6], [10..11],
  [6], [`    para`], [0], [7], [11..12],
)

Block 4's `subtree_end` is 7 while block 2's is 3: the second item holds two
paragraphs, the first holds one, and both are found by the same hop. Item one's
child is `plain` and item two's are `para` — the tight-versus-loose distinction
pandoc draws, which a writer needs in order to reproduce blank lines
faithfully.

#tbl(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, auto, auto),
  align: (right, left, left, right),
  table.header([*j*], [*inline*], [*data / attr*], [*subtree#linebreak()end*]),
  [0], [`str`], [`"The"`], [1],
  [1], [`space`], [—], [2],
  [2], [`emph`], [—], [4],
  [3], [`  str`], [`"quick"`], [4],
  [4], [`space`], [—], [5],
  [5], [`link`], [`links[0]`], [7],
  [6], [`  str`], [`"brown"`], [7],
  [7], [`space`], [—], [8],
  [8], [`str`], [`"fox"`], [9],
)

The heading's `inlines` range is 0..9. Its seven top-level children are the
ones reachable by hopping from 0, which skips the children of `emph` and `link`
without a test for what they are.

== Why the tree is a tree

An earlier draft of this record proposed a flat inline layer: a sequence of
text runs each carrying a style bitset, with no inline nesting. The argument
was that the office formats contain no inline nesting to preserve — DOCX runs
carry independent property flags, and so do ODT spans and RTF groups — so
building a tree from flags means inventing structure the source never
specified.

That argument is right about the readers and wrong about the system, for three
reasons.

*Filters need node identity.* A transform that wraps every `code` inline in a
`link` to an API reference has to put a node around another node. A bitset can
be set and cleared; it cannot be wrapped. Every interesting filter — unwrap
this container, wrap that one, replace a node with a subtree — is a structural
edit, and a flat run layer offers nothing to edit. Since filters are a first
requirement rather than a later addition, this alone settles it.

*Not every inline container is a style.* `link`, `image`, `note`, `span`, and
`cite` carry payloads and children that no flag set can express. Once the
representation must nest for those, making `emph` and `strong` different in
kind buys nothing and costs a special case in every walker.

*Compatibility with pandoc's model has value the flat form forfeits.* A
document model people already know is easier to write plugins against, easier
to explain, and leaves a JSON bridge to the existing filter ecosystem as a
tractable later option rather than a rewrite.

What the flat-run argument does establish is a requirement on readers, and it
is kept: *a reader converting a flag set to nesting must use a canonical
order*, so bold-italic text produces the same tree no matter which flag the
source listed first. The order is `link`, `strong`, `emph`, `strikeout`,
`superscript`, `subscript`, `small_caps`, `underline`, outermost first. It is
stated once, here, and asserted by a shared test that every flag-based reader
runs.

== The Builder

Readers and filters do not touch `Document`. They append through a `Builder`,
which owns the growable arrays, the interning table, and the open-node stack.

```zig
pub const Builder = struct {
    blocks: std.MultiArrayList(Block) = .empty,
    inlines: std.MultiArrayList(Inline) = .empty,
    text: std.ArrayList(u8) = .empty,
    attrs: std.ArrayList(Attr) = .empty,
    // ... side tables, the intern map, the open-node stack

    /// Opens a container block; must be matched by `closeBlock`.
    pub fn openBlock(b: *Builder, gpa: Allocator, tag: BlockTag, a: Attrs) !u32;
    pub fn closeBlock(b: *Builder, gpa: Allocator) void;
    pub fn leafBlock(b: *Builder, gpa: Allocator, tag: BlockTag, a: Attrs) !u32;

    /// Opens a container inline; must be matched by `closeInline`.
    pub fn openInline(b: *Builder, gpa: Allocator, tag: InlineTag, a: Attrs) !u32;
    pub fn closeInline(b: *Builder, gpa: Allocator) void;

    /// Appends text, splitting on whitespace into `str` and `space` nodes and
    /// coalescing with a preceding `str`, so a reader appending arbitrary
    /// chunks still produces a canonical tree.
    pub fn text(b: *Builder, gpa: Allocator, bytes: []const u8) !void;

    /// Copies a whole subtree from another document: the fast path behind
    /// filter subtree sharing. One `memcpy` per array plus an index rebase.
    pub fn copyBlocks(b: *Builder, gpa: Allocator, from: *const Document, root: u32) !u32;
    pub fn copyInlines(b: *Builder, gpa: Allocator, from: *const Document, root: u32) !u32;

    /// Freezes into a `Document`. Asserts the open stack is empty.
    pub fn finish(b: *Builder) Document;
};
```

`text` is the one place the `Builder` is opinionated, and deliberately: the
whitespace and coalescing invariants are easier to hold in one function than in
nine readers.

`finish` asserts the open stack is empty. A plugin that returns unbalanced is a
bug, not a recoverable condition, and it is caught in that plugin's own tests
rather than papered over by an auto-close.

= Traversal and Rewriting Algorithms

The storage was chosen for these. This section states what it buys and what
each operation costs.

== Bounded, non-recursive walking

No traversal in zenfmt recurses. Every walker keeps an explicit stack of open
node indices bounded by `limits.max_depth`, checked on push. This follows the
coding standard's prohibition on recursion, and it is a safety rule before it
is a style rule: nesting depth is attacker-controlled, and a bounded stack
turns a stack overflow into a report.

The common traversal does not need the stack at all. Emitting a document in
document order is a single forward scan over the array, because preorder
storage *is* document order. The stack is needed only for writers that must
emit something on the way out of a node, and those keep one entry per open
container.

== Skipping and scanning

Two operations a pointer-based tree cannot do cheaply:

*Subtree skip.* A walker that decides a node is uninteresting sets
`i = subtree_end[i]` and continues. The entire branch costs nothing — not one
cache line is touched.

*Tag scan.* Because storage is struct-of-arrays, `blocks.items(.tag)` is a
contiguous `[]BlockTag`, one byte per node. Finding every table in a document
is `std.mem.indexOfScalar` over that column: a vectorized byte scan at memory
bandwidth, with no pointer chasing and no node loads. Filters that match a
single tag are implemented this way, and the pipeline recognizes them — a
filter declaring `pub const matches: []const BlockTag = &.{.table}` gets the
scan path rather than the walk path.

== The rebuild transform, with subtree sharing

Filters do not mutate. A stage reads one `Document` and writes another into the
same arena. The naive cost of that is a full copy per stage, and the algorithm
that avoids it is why this record cares about storage layout.

The transform is a post-order pass returning, for each node, whether anything
beneath it changed:

+ Visit the node's children first, recording whether any child was rewritten.
+ Call the filter's `visitBlock` or `visitInline` for this node.
+ If the filter returned `keep` *and* no descendant changed, emit the node by
  copying its whole subtree from the source: one `memcpy` into each array, plus
  a rebase of `subtree_end` and the `Range` fields by the destination offset.
  The children are neither visited again nor re-emitted one at a time.
+ Otherwise emit the node, then the rewritten children individually.

The consequences, stated as costs, since "fast" is not a design:

#tbl(
  columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
  table.header([*Case*], [*Cost*], [*Note*]),
  [A filter that matches nothing],
  [one `memcpy` per array],
  [The root reports no change, so the whole document copies as one contiguous
    block. An identity filter runs at memory bandwidth.],

  [A filter that rewrites `k` nodes],
  [`O(k · d + m)`],
  [`d` is depth, `m` the total size of the `k` rewritten subtrees. Only the
    spine from each rewritten node to the root is rebuilt node by node; every
    sibling branch is a `memcpy`.],

  [A tag-scan filter],
  [`O(n)` bytes `+` the above],
  [The scan reads one byte per node, not twenty-one.],

  [A pipeline of `s` filters],
  [`s` passes],
  [Each produces a new tree in the arena. Nothing is freed between stages; the
    arena is released once at the end.],
)

The rebase step deserves a note because a bug there would be silent. A copied
subtree's internal indices are all relative to the source array; adding the
destination offset to `subtree_end` and to every `Range` pointing within the
copied region is correct precisely because a subtree is *contiguous and
self-contained* — no node inside it can point outside it except through the
side tables, which are copied wholesale and never renumbered. The validator
runs after every stage, so a rebase error fails immediately rather than
producing a plausible wrong document.

== Complexity summary

#tbl(
  columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
  table.header([*Operation*], [*Cost*], [*Why*]),
  [Read a node], [`O(1)`], [Array index.],
  [Iterate children], [`O(children)`], [Hop `subtree_end`.],
  [Skip a subtree], [`O(1)`], [Assign `subtree_end`.],
  [Find all nodes of a tag], [`O(n)` bytes], [Vectorized scan of the tag column.],
  [Copy a subtree], [`O(size)` `memcpy`], [Contiguous.],
  [Full traversal], [`O(n)`], [Linear scan; preorder is document order.],
  [Filter stage, `k` edits], [`O(k · d + m)`], [Spine rebuild plus edited subtrees.],
  [Depth of any operation], [`≤ max_depth`], [Checked, not assumed.],
