= Filters

== The `build.zig` analogy, taken literally

Zig's build system is configured by a Zig file in the user's project that
imports `std.Build`, declares a graph through a typed API, and is compiled and
run by the toolchain. It is not a configuration format and not a scripting
language; it is a program, with a compiler checking it.

zenfmt filters work the same way. A user writes a Zig file that imports the
`zenfmt` module, declares a pipeline through a typed API, and compiles it into
a binary:

```zig
// filters.zig, in the user's own project
const std = @import("std");
const zenfmt = @import("zenfmt");

pub fn filters(p: *zenfmt.Pipeline) void {
    // One that ships with zenfmt.
    p.add(zenfmt.filters.shift_headings, .{ .by = 1 });

    // One the user wrote, in this file or beside it.
    p.add(InternalLinks, .{ .base = "https://docs.example.com/" });

    // Order matters, and is exactly the order written here.
    p.add(zenfmt.filters.drop_empty_divs, .{});
}

const InternalLinks = struct {
    pub const name = "internal-links";
    pub const description = "Rewrite bare `#anchor` targets to absolute URLs";
    pub const matches: []const zenfmt.InlineTag = &.{.link};

    base: []const u8,

    pub fn visitInline(
        f: *@This(),
        ctx: *zenfmt.Filter.Context,
        node: u32,
    ) !zenfmt.Filter.Action {
        const target = ctx.linkTarget(node);
        if (!std.mem.startsWith(u8, target, "#")) return .keep;

        const absolute = try ctx.fmt("{s}{s}", .{ f.base, target[1..] });
        try ctx.openLink(absolute, ctx.linkTitle(node));
        try ctx.emitChildren(node);
        ctx.close();
        return .replaced;
    }
};
```

```sh
zig build           # produces a zenfmt binary carrying these filters
zenfmt --filters manual.docx -o manual.md
```

Everything a filter does is type-checked, there is no serialization boundary,
and a filter costs a direct call — the pipeline is an `inline for` over the
registered stages. The tradeoff is stated plainly under _Alternatives
Considered_: this buys speed and safety at the cost of requiring a Zig
toolchain to write a filter, where pandoc requires only a Lua interpreter.

== The filter contract

A filter is a Zig type with a name, optional options fields, and one or both
visit methods.

```zig
pub const Filter = struct {
    pub const Order = enum {
        /// Children are visited before their parent. The default, and what a
        /// filter that rewrites leaves into containers wants.
        bottom_up,
        /// The parent is visited first; returning `.drop` or `.replaced`
        /// means its children are never visited.
        top_down,
    };

    pub const Action = enum {
        /// Emit this node unchanged, with its rewritten children. When no
        /// descendant changed, this is the `memcpy` fast path.
        keep,
        /// Emit nothing: the node and its subtree disappear.
        drop,
        /// Emit the node's children in its place, without the node itself.
        unwrap,
        /// The filter has already emitted a replacement into `ctx`.
        replaced,
    };

    pub const Context = struct {
        gpa: std.mem.Allocator,
        /// The tree being read. Immutable for the duration of the stage.
        in: *const Document,
        /// The tree being written. Filters emit replacements here.
        out: *Builder,
        reports: *Reports,
        limits: Limits,

        // Inspection: O(1), or a bounded scan.
        pub fn blockTag(ctx: *Context, node: u32) BlockTag;
        pub fn inlineTag(ctx: *Context, node: u32) InlineTag;
        pub fn textOf(ctx: *Context, node: u32) []const u8;
        pub fn hasClass(ctx: *Context, node: u32, class: []const u8) bool;
        pub fn attribute(ctx: *Context, node: u32, key: []const u8) ?[]const u8;
        pub fn children(ctx: *Context, node: u32) Children;

        // Emission, for the `.replaced` action.
        pub fn emitChildren(ctx: *Context, node: u32) !void;
        pub fn emitCopy(ctx: *Context, node: u32) !void;
        pub fn emitStr(ctx: *Context, bytes: []const u8) !void;
        pub fn open(ctx: *Context, tag: InlineTag, a: Attrs) !void;
        pub fn close(ctx: *Context) void;
        // ... and the block equivalents

        /// Formats into the arena. The result lives as long as the tree.
        pub fn fmt(ctx: *Context, comptime f: []const u8, args: anytype) ![]const u8;

        /// Reports a transformation the user should know about, in the same
        /// Elm form readers and writers use.
        pub fn report(ctx: *Context, r: Report) !void;
    };
};
```

The contract:

- *A filter may assume* the input tree satisfies every invariant, that `in`
  does not change while the stage runs, and that the arena outlives the call.
- *A filter must* leave the builder balanced, respect `limits`, and be
  deterministic — the same input tree must produce the same output tree.
- *A filter must not* write to the output stream, read the filesystem or the
  network, depend on the input or output format, or retain an index from `in`
  past the end of its stage.
- *The engine guarantees* it validates the tree after every stage, so a filter
  producing a structurally invalid tree fails at its own stage rather than
  corrupting a later one.

The prohibition on format knowledge is the important one. A filter that behaves
differently for DOCX input is a reader bug wearing a disguise; the whole point
of the tree is that a transform written against it works for every format that
can reach it.

== Filters that ship

A small set, chosen because they are the transforms people actually reach for
and because each exercises a different part of the contract:

